{"folioDeck": 1, "exportedAt": 1793318400000, "meta": {"id": "jahira", "title": "Japanese — Hiragana", "subtitle": "The 105 hiragana — start here, before any word", "desc": "The first thing to learn in Japanese, before any word: hiragana, the syllabary Japanese uses for its grammar — particles, verb endings — and for words with no kanji, and the script of every reading. 105 notes, each with TWO cards (hiragana → romaji, romaji → hiragana), each scheduled on its own: the 46 of the basic chart, the 25 voiced with ゛ and ゜, the small っ that doubles a consonant, and the 33 combinations with a small ゃ, ゅ or ょ — in that order, with the 9 a beginner can leave for last at the end (ぢ and づ, almost always written じ and ず, and the combinations no word of the JLPT lists contains). The romaji is Hepburn. Three sounds are written two ways (じ and ぢ, ず and づ, お and を), and the card for the rarer one says which it asks for. The back shows the matching katakana, the kana it is most easily mistaken for (ぬ and め, は and ほ), a note where the kana is not said as it is spelled (は and へ as particles, う and い lengthening a vowel), and a word from the JLPT N5 and N4 decks written in kana with the kana picked out, its usual spelling beside its meaning. At the app's default for a language deck, ten new cards a day with both directions together, that is five kana a day and the 210 cards take about 21 days; raising this deck's daily new-card limit to fifteen or twenty in its options brings that to 14 or 11. A kana's reading card (hiragana → romaji) and its writing card arrive together unless the deck's option to deal both directions together is switched off. Study before the first words and JLPT N5. — Example words: Jonathan Waller's JLPT lists (CC BY) and JMdict (EDRDG, CC BY-SA 4.0).", "author": "", "language": "en", "color": "#BC002D", "tags": ["japanese", "hiragana", "kana", "writing"], "glossMode": "site", "types": {"kana": {"id": "kana", "name": "Japanese kana", "speechLang": "ja-JP", "field